  "image_name": "7a3898f8de72b3c0a0bdc44de5537169.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual summary of how a tree moves from normal function to death as drought and warming intensify. Across the top, an arrow shows increasing mortality risk, while the tree diagrams progress from a healthy pre-drought state to shutdown, permanent damage, and finally dying.\n\nThe main idea is that water loss from the canopy rises first, triggering stomatal closure and a sharp reduction in gas exchange. As drought deepens, water supply from soil and fine roots declines, the soil–root connection weakens, and the plant loses turgor. Blue circles representing water pools and green circles for non-structural carbon (NSC) pools both shrink over time, indicating depletion of hydraulic and carbon reserves. Carbon use is increasingly restricted: early on, carbon supports growth, defence, respiration, and symbionts, but later only defence and respiration remain, and eventually mainly respiration.\n\nThe diagram also links hydraulic failure and carbon limitation. It highlights processes such as NSC-based osmoregulation, hydraulic resistance, runaway cavitation, phloem transport failure, and meristem death. Together these changes suggest that drought mortality is not caused by one mechanism alone, but by interacting failures in water transport, carbon allocation, and tissue survival.",
